17592186354311 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354311 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $B$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186354311 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cffbdb26-6f7f-5724-84b4-58cbeec7ece4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354311 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-5I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-3)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{b